## Measuring Contract Overhead

### Writing a Benchmark
```xiom
#[bench]
fn bench_with_contracts() -> Int {
  var sum = 0;
  for i in 0..1000000 {
    sum = sum + safe_divide(1000, 10);
  }
  return sum;
}

#[bench]
fn bench_without_contracts() -> Int {
  var sum = 0;
  for i in 0..1000000 {
    sum = sum + unsafe_divide(1000, 10);
  }
  return sum;
}
```

### Running Benchmarks
```sh
# Run all benchmarks
xiom bench

# Run specific benchmark
xiom bench -- bench_with_contracts

# Compare two builds
xiom bench --compare debug release
```

### Interpreting Results
```
bench_with_contracts     time: [1.234 ns 1.245 ns 1.256 ns]
bench_without_contracts  time: [1.201 ns 1.210 ns 1.219 ns]

Contract overhead: ~2.9% (35 ps per call)
```

### When to Strip Contracts

- **Always keep**: Public API, safety-critical functions
- **Consider stripping**: Hot loops (>1M calls/sec), internal helpers
- **Benchmark first**: 2-3% overhead on simple checks, <0.1% on complex functions

### The Golden Rule
Don't strip contracts for performance until you've MEASURED the impact. Most contracts cost less than you think.

